# Element Armory — Server build phases

Phased todo derived from server-setup.md. Complete phases in order; later phases depend on earlier ones.

---

## Phase 1: Setup — project init, structure, DB, Express API

- [ ] Initialize Node.js project in `./server` (package.json, TypeScript, tsconfig).
- [ ] Install and wire core packages: express, typescript, ts-node-dev (or similar), dotenv.
- [ ] Create folder structure: `/src/api`, `/src/api/middleware`, `/src/api/routes`, `/src/config`, `/src/db`, `/src/db/migrations`, `/src/db/schema`, `/src/loaders`, `/src/models`, `/src/services`, `/src/jobs`, `/src/public`, `/src/types`, `/src/utils`, `/src/constants`.
- [ ] Add DB layer: SQLite connection (`/src/db/connection.ts`), config for DB path/env.
- [ ] Add migration runner and first migration (schema version or placeholder). Design for future Postgres (e.g. avoid SQLite-specific types where possible).
- [ ] Bootstrap Express in `/src/loaders/express.ts`: JSON body, CORS, health route.
- [ ] Add `/src/api/index.ts` to mount routes and wire Express app.
- [ ] Add minimal health/readiness endpoints (e.g. `GET /health`, `GET /ready`).
- [ ] Add `/src/constants` and `/src/types` with shared types (e.g. env vars, app constants).
- [ ] Document run commands (e.g. `npm run dev`, `npm run migrate`) in README.

---

## Phase 2: Identity, account creation, auth, and hosted app

- [ ] Define install identity model: `install_id` (UUID/ULID), `install_secret` (backend-issued). No machine fingerprinting as identity.
- [ ] Add `installs` table (and migrations): install_id, install_secret, user_id (nullable), created_at, last_seen_at; optional telemetry columns (extension_version, chrome_version, os_family, etc.).
- [ ] Implement install registration API: extension sends install_id (and optional telemetry); backend issues and stores install_secret, returns it to extension.
- [ ] Add Better Auth: install and configure in `/src/loaders/auth.ts`. Primary: Google OAuth. Secondary: magic link via email.
- [ ] Configure email sending with **AWS SES**. Add SES client and env vars (region, credentials).
- [ ] Add **react-email** (or compatible) email templates; wire SES to send magic-link and any verification emails.
- [ ] Create core auth tables (or let Better Auth own them); ensure `users` and `sessions` (or equivalent) exist. Keep app tables (installs, captures) separate.
- [ ] Implement “link install to user” on sign-in: when user signs in on hosted app, associate current install with user_id (one install ↔ one account at a time).
- [ ] Hosted app (e.g. Next.js or static + API): minimal pages for sign-in callback, account page, and links to billing. Base URL e.g. `https://app.elementarmory.com`.
- [ ] **Chrome extension auth**: design and implement long-lived extension-safe session (app-issued token for user + install). Extension opens hosted sign-in; after auth, exchange for token; implement silent refresh and sign-out that revokes extension session.
- [ ] API routes: install registration, auth callback, session exchange for extension, link/unlink install from account (from account page).

---

## Phase 3: Stripe integration and entitlements

- [ ] Add Stripe SDK and `/src/loaders/stripe.ts`; env for Stripe keys and webhook secret.
- [ ] Define internal **plans** (e.g. free, pro, team) and **states** (active, inactive, trialing, past_due, canceled). Add `subscriptions` (or entitlement cache) and `stripe_customers` tables; add `subscription_events` for audit.
- [ ] Implement **subscription sync service**: map Stripe webhook events (and optional API polling) into app entitlements. Rest of app checks entitlements, not raw Stripe.
- [ ] **Stripe Hosted Checkout**: “Upgrade” flow — create Checkout Session, redirect user to Stripe; on success redirect to hosted app success URL. Require account before purchase.
- [ ] **Stripe Customer Portal**: “Manage billing” — generate Portal session URL, redirect user. No custom invoice/subscription UIs.
- [ ] Webhooks (from day one): endpoint for Stripe events; verify signature; handle at least: checkout.session.completed, customer.subscription.created/updated/deleted, invoice.paid, invoice.payment_failed. Provision or revoke access via subscription sync service.
- [ ] API routes: create checkout session, create portal session, webhook handler. Hosted app pages: Upgrade button → Checkout; Manage billing → Portal.

---

## Phase 4: Element sync and storage

- [ ] Define **capture** model (metadata only in DB): install_id, user_id (nullable), source_url, captured_at (UTC), created_by_install_id, status, metadata. No raw screenshots in DB.
- [ ] Define **capture_assets**: references to object storage (screenshot key, HTML ref, stylesheet ref, checksums). Table links capture_id to asset keys/URLs.
- [ ] Add S3 (or compatible) client and config; bucket(s) for screenshots and optional large HTML/CSS blobs.
- [ ] Implement upload flow: extension uploads screenshot (and optionally HTML/CSS) to presigned URL or via API; API creates capture record and capture_assets records with object keys.
- [ ] Enforce payload size limits and screenshot upload limits in API.
- [ ] Implement capture and capture_assets services (create, list by install, list by user after link). Ensure anonymous captures (install-only) stay intact and become visible when install is linked to user.
- [ ] Add `/src/jobs` structure: placeholders or minimal jobs for asset verification, S3 backup, or future MCP enrichment. Nightly DB backup to S3 (optional in this phase).
- [ ] API routes: submit capture (install-authenticated), list captures (by install or by user when linked).

---

## Phase 5: Rate limits and abuse protection

- [ ] Per-install rate limiting: throttle by install_id (and validate install_secret on each request).
- [ ] Request signing or install_secret validation on all install-scoped endpoints (sensitive and sync).
- [ ] Payload size limits (body and per-asset) and screenshot upload limits already considered in Phase 4; enforce and document.
- [ ] User-Agent validation: expect extension identity; optional block or throttle unknown UAs.
- [ ] Optional: per extension_version checks (e.g. warn or throttle very old versions).
- [ ] Optional: proof-of-install token issued by backend for extra assurance.
- [ ] Add middleware in `/src/api/middleware` for rate limit and install auth; apply to capture and install routes.

---

## Phase 6: MCP server (authenticated) and “Copy MCP” in extension

- [ ] Add MCP server package and run it on a **different port** from the Express API. Keep it a thin layer: MCP tools call the same `/src/services` (and models) the API uses.
- [ ] Design MCP auth: only authenticated users (and optionally install) can use MCP. Issue or accept app-issued tokens; validate on each MCP request.
- [ ] Implement MCP tools that delegate to existing services (e.g. list captures, get capture details, search). No duplicate business logic.
- [ ] Structure so MCP can be moved to a separate microservice later (no tight coupling to Express app).
- [ ] **Chrome extension**: add “Copy MCP” (or “MCP connection”) entry point: generate link or code snippet (e.g. URL + token or config) that users can paste into their MCP client (Cursor, etc.). Ensure token is scoped and revocable.
- [ ] Document in app or extension how to add Element Armory MCP to an MCP client.

---

## Phase 7: Hosted app polish and operations (optional)

- [ ] Hosted app: library/history of captures view; account page shows linked installs and unlink option.
- [ ] Sign-out on install: remove account link from install; captures remain on install.
- [ ] Nightly DB backup to S3; retention policy.
- [ ] Monitoring and logging for API and MCP (e.g. request logs, error tracking).
- [ ] README and env example for full server setup (DB, SES, S3, Stripe, Better Auth, MCP).

---

## Summary checklist (from server-setup.md)

- Install ID + install secret; no fingerprinting as identity.
- Captures belong to install first, user second; link on sign-in.
- Stripe Hosted Checkout + Customer Portal; provisioning via webhooks.
- Screenshots in S3; DB holds metadata and references only.
- Rate limiting and install_secret for anonymous install sync.
- Migrations and jobs folders from the start.
- MCP as thin layer over shared services; separate port.
- Small hosted account app for auth, billing, and library.
